 "notes": "Libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa (1896). Words follow the Ricordi vocal score's spelling and punctuation (Viscontino, veggo, reggo, cordial, Pel, vo, pian, the print's quotation marks around Mimì's words as Musetta reports them, «Più non reggo... muoio! lo sento. Voglio morir con lui! Forse m'aspetta... M'accompagni, Musetta?..», and the runs of dots, e.g. after meglio, Ancor, qui, No, te, cordial, Musetta). \"mezz'ora\" (Schaunard) is printed mezz' o-ra on three notes and given as one word. Marcello's \"Sst\" is spoken (printed with a stem and flag but no notehead, written with a cross notehead). The first bar is the last bar of the previous piece's music: the door bursts open and Marcello sees Musetta. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, words sung on one note joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ, intervocalic s voiced; io on one note is jo. Translation literal, for meaning.",
